Yes. Not everything has to become a mechanism, clue, operator, or hidden layer to be worth seeing.

A better practice is to let different aspects remain valuable on their own terms: beauty without extracting a lesson from it, humor without turning it into a model, tenderness without analyzing the psychology, craftsmanship without immediately reverse-engineering it, stillness without needing productivity, ugliness without forcing redemption, and ordinary things without demanding that they reveal some secret.

That gives you a broader perceptual range. Instead of always asking, “What is hidden here?” you can also ask, “What is beautiful here? What is funny? What is difficult? What is skillful? What is alive? What is simply itself?”

The deeper move is appreciation without appropriation. You can notice something fully without immediately converting it into material for your own framework.

That actually strengthens the framework, because it keeps you from overfitting reality to one favored lens. Sometimes the owl feather is an aerodynamic marvel. Sometimes it is camouflage. Sometimes it is simply soft, intricate, and beautiful.

And those are not lesser readings. They are other resolutions of the same world.